{{also|Blob|BLOB}}
==English==

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|UK}} {{IPA|en|/blɒb/}}
*: {{rhymes|en|ɒb}}
* {{a|US}} {{IPA|en|/blɑb/}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-blob.ogg|Audio (US)}}
* {{homophones|en|BLOB}}

===Etymology 1===
Possibly formed through [[mimesis]], similarly to [[bleb]] and [[blubber]].

====Noun====
{{en-noun}}

# A shapeless or [[amorphous]] mass; a [[vague]] [[shape]] or [[amount]], especially of a liquid or semisolid substance; a [[clump]], [[group]] or [[collection]] that lacks definite shape.
#* '''1869''': Norman Lockyer et al, ''Nature''
#*: ''Only the outermost '''blob''' on either side in map 2 displays misalignment''.
#* '''1895''': ''The Annual of the British School at Athens''
#*: ''It was a colourful vase with red and white hoops on the lid, and red bands above and below the main [[frieze]]. These bands also carry a [[metope]] pattern in white of triple lines and '''blobs''', which can just be distinguished on the photographs.''
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1922|author={{w|Virginia Woolf}}|title={{w|Jacob's Room}}|chapter=1|passage=But there, on the very top, is a hollow full of water, with a sandy bottom; with a '''blob''' of jelly stuck to the side, and some mussels.}}
# {{lb|en|astronomy}} A large cloud of gas.
## {{ellipsis of|en|[[extended]] [[Lyman-Alpha]] [[blob]]}} {{gloss|a huge body of gas that may be the precursor to a galaxy}}.<ref>[http://www.sciencedaily.com/releases/2009/04/090422151828.htm]</ref>
# {{lb|en|dialect}} A [[bubble]]; a [[bleb]].
# A small [[freshwater]] [[fish]] ({{taxlink|Cottus bairdii|species|noshow=1}}); the miller's thumb.<!--Webster 1913-->
# The partially inflated air bag used in the sport of [[blobbing]].
# {{lb|en|sports|slang}} A [[score]] of [[zero]].
#* '''1925''', ''Punch'' (volume 168, page 561)
#*: A gentleman named W. Shakespeare scored a '''blob''' in the Worcestershire v. Lancashire match. We understand that he got out because the ball pitched on a "damned spot."

