====Verb====
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|transitive}} To drop in the form of a blob or blobs
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1917|author={{w|Edgar Wallace}}|title=The Keepers of the King's Peace|chapter=6|url=http://gutenberg.net.au/ebooks06/0600061h.html|passage=Bones put the tiny crimson speck between his slides, '''blobbed''' a drop of oil on top, and focused the microscope.}}
#* '''1957''', "War of Nerves," ''[[w:Time (magazine)|Time]]'', 7 October, 1957, [http://www.time.com/time/magazine/article/0,9171,809951,00.html]
#*: {{...}} a cross has been burned during the night on Wechsler's lawn and a painted KKK '''blobbed''' across one wall of his home.
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To drop a blob or blobs onto, cover with blobs.
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1941|author={{w|Emily Carr}}|title={{w|Klee Wyck}}|chapter=20|url=http://www.gutenberg.net.au/ebooks01/0100131h.html|passage=She was beating something in a pail, beating it with her hands; her arms were '''blobbed''' with pink froth to the elbows.}}
#* '''1959''', "The Big Appel," ''[[w:Time (magazine)|Time]]'', 7 December, 1959, [http://www.time.com/time/magazine/article/0,9171,811550,00.html]
#*: Asked to do a mural in the coffee room of the Municipal Museum, [[w:Karel Appel|Appel]] responded by '''blobbing''' all four walls and the ceiling with brilliant colors {{...}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To fall in the form of a blob or blobs.
#* '''1964''', {{w|A. S. Byatt}}, ''The Shadow of the Sun'', Harcourt, Brace & Co., 1991, Chapter Three, p. 47,
#*: Caroline began to separate eggs, cracking them into unbelievably even halves, sliding the gold, round and elastic, from shell to shell, whilst the white hung, heavy, translucent, in thick sheets, and '''blobbed''' suddenly into her basin.
#* '''2013''', Marcus Berkmann, "Blood and gore of the real 'who dunnits'," Review of ''Silent Witnesses'' by {{w|Nigel McCrery}}, ''{{w|Daily Mail}}'', 22 August, 2013, [http://www.dailymail.co.uk/home/books/article-2400118/Blood-gore-real-dunnits-SILENT-WITNESSES-BY-NIGEL-MCCRERY.html]
#*: {{...}} whether the blood has splashed, or '''blobbed''', or trickled, can reveal whether the victim was killed here or moved afterwards.
# {{lb|en|intransitive|slang}} To [[relax]] [[idle|idly]] and [[mindless]]ly; to [[veg out]].

